# IBM Verify tenant credentials for the Verify IdP path.
#
#   cp .env.verify.example .env.verify    # then fill in the secret below
#
# restart.sh sources ./.env.verify when it exists, so the value reaches the
# gateway process. .env.verify is gitignored — these are live tenant
# credentials and do not belong in the repo. (The Keycloak configs keep their
# secrets inline in YAML, which is safe only because those are throwaway
# localhost credentials.)
#
# This is the EXCHANGE client (praxis-gateway's Verify counterpart), which is
# deliberately not the client that mints user tokens — Verify refuses to let a
# client exchange its own tokens (CSIAQ5207E). Client id
# c619871a-6a66-45cd-b1eb-dddde1c318c2 is in policy-verify-opa.yaml; only the
# secret lives here.
#
# Find it in the Verify admin console under
#   Applications → <the gateway app> → Sign-on → Client secret
VERIFY_GATEWAY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The minting client's secret is read separately by mint-verify-token.sh from
# post_deploy_variables.json (HR_COPILOT_CLIENT_SECRET), which is also
# gitignored. Nothing to set here for minting.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Admin API client — only needed to PROVISION users, not to run the demo
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# verify/import-verify-users.sh uses these to create the four demo personas and
# their custom attributes from verify/users.csv, and
# verify/import-verify-clients.sh uses them to create the OAuth clients from
# verify/clients.csv. Leave them blank unless you are building or rebuilding a
# tenant; the walkthrough itself never reads them.
#
# This is deliberately a THIRD client, separate from the minting and exchange
# clients above. Those two are in the demo's hot path, and a client that can
# mint alice's token should not also be able to rewrite alice's permissions.
#
# Create it under Security -> API access -> Add API client, and grant these three
# entitlements (Verify checks entitlements per endpoint, not OAuth scopes):
#   "Manage users and groups"      (manageUsers)          -> POST/DELETE /v2.0/Users
#   "Manage attributes"            (manageAttributes)     -> POST /v1.0/attributes
#   "Manage application lifecycle" (manageAppAccessAdmin) -> /v1.0/applications
#
# The third is only needed by verify/import-verify-clients.sh, which creates the
# demo's OAuth clients. A 403 from that script with a valid token means exactly
# that entitlement is missing.
VERIFY_ADMIN_CLIENT_ID=
VERIFY_ADMIN_CLIENT_SECRET=
